    {
      "id": "3Ywy9H5JT87LVETZIOzCX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 873.773386832589,
      "y": 1858.787849678748,
      "width": 812.5795288085938,
      "height": 1050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1H",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 725582173,
      "version": 769,
      "versionNonce": 822235837,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785727681561,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We need to \n1. Go to location name ===>  polygon\n2. Use that polygon to filter businesses that exist within it\n\nTo solve for #1, we need to create new table that map location names to polygons\n(can base on public dataset like Geoapify to get this)\na/ Create a locations table with columns for name (e.g., \"San Francisco\"),\n type (e.g., \"city\", \"neighborhood\"), and polygon (to store the geographic data).\nb/ Populate this table with data from the chosen sources.\nc/ Index the name column for efficient lookups.\n\nTo solve for #2, Elasticsearch has functionality working with polygon \n(Geoshape)\n\n// For elasticsearch, add new geo_shape field to document\n{\n  \"query\": {\n    \"geo_bounding_box\": { \n      \"location\": {\n        \"top_left\": {\n          \"lat\": 42,\n          \"lon\": -72\n        },\n        \"bottom_right\": {\n          \"lat\": 40,\n          \"lon\": -74\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n{\n  \"id\": \"123\",\n  \"name\": \"Pizza Place\",\n  \"location_names\": [\"bay_area\",\"san_francisco\", \"mission_district\"],\n  \"category\": \"restaurant\"\n}\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We need to \n1. Go to location name ===>  polygon\n2. Use that polygon to filter businesses that exist within it\n\nTo solve for #1, we need to create new table that map location names to polygons\n(can base on public dataset like Geoapify to get this)\na/ Create a locations table with columns for name (e.g., \"San Francisco\"),\n type (e.g., \"city\", \"neighborhood\"), and polygon (to store the geographic data).\nb/ Populate this table with data from the chosen sources.\nc/ Index the name column for efficient lookups.\n\nTo solve for #2, Elasticsearch has functionality working with polygon \n(Geoshape)\n\n// For elasticsearch, add new geo_shape field to document\n{\n  \"query\": {\n    \"geo_bounding_box\": { \n      \"location\": {\n        \"top_left\": {\n          \"lat\": 42,\n          \"lon\": -72\n        },\n        \"bottom_right\": {\n          \"lat\": 40,\n          \"lon\": -74\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n{\n  \"id\": \"123\",\n  \"name\": \"Pizza Place\",\n  \"location_names\": [\"bay_area\",\"san_francisco\", \"mission_district\"],\n  \"category\": \"restaurant\"\n}\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
